1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
These are earth-shaking fires.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
A special breed of firefighters snuffs them.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Specialists who blow out raging infernos with explosives

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:21,000
and cap fountains of highly flammable oil or gas without blowing themselves up.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, oil firefighting on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,720
Welcome to disaster.

7
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:44,520
It doesn't get any worse than a raging oil or gas well fire.

8
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,640
Choking, burning smoke shoots hundreds of feet into the air.

9
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:55,280
Without protection, the heat can cook a person's skin several yards from the blaze.

10
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,860
But once it's out, it's even more dangerous.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,380
the specialists work to cap the blowing well under a shower of flammable crew

12
00:01:05,381 --> 00:01:12,700
standing beside a blowing well is like placing your head next to a jet engine only louder

13
00:01:12,701 --> 00:01:20,200
they're so loud that you put cotton and vaseline in your ears and then you shove an ear plug in

14
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:28,180
behind that only a handful of men in the entire world are qualified to put out a fire like this

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,960
You got to be careful that nobody makes a mistake.

16
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,780
It's not just jumping and do this and do that.

17
00:01:36,940 --> 00:01:38,880
There's a lot more to it than people think.

18
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:43,020
Every well fire presents unique problems,

19
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,300
but the basics of the job are always the same.

20
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,980
Put out the fire and cap the blowing well.

21
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:59,680
To avoid being incinerated while doing their incredibly dangerous work,

22
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,220
Experts use specialized tools and methods perfected over the last 70 years.

23
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:14,320
First of all, you have to gain ready access, safe access for the people to do the work.

24
00:02:14,321 --> 00:02:25,560
If there was a drilling rig and it caught fire and is burnt up, it means first of all the safe removal of all that debris from around the well.

25
00:02:25,561 --> 00:02:32,040
Firefighters use bulldozers to drag away the twisted wreckage of a burnt-up rig.

26
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:41,520
They also use an A-fee wagon, an A-frame trailer with a long boom and a hook on the end, to grab debris.

27
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:50,960
So we have to take it away a piece at a time, because if we do put it out, one hot piece of metal could reignite the well.

28
00:02:50,961 --> 00:02:57,980
giant pumps spray a deluge of water over men and equipment protecting them from the intense heat

29
00:02:57,981 --> 00:03:03,740
this is one of our bigger pumps in our inventory this is a

30
00:03:03,741 --> 00:03:12,640
5 000 gallons per minute pump you got the engine as your driver the transmission and the pump itself

31
00:03:12,641 --> 00:03:18,180
often the only thing between these firefighters and a raging inferno

32
00:03:18,181 --> 00:03:20,620
is a 1 8th inch piece of tin.

33
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,600
Well, out of all the equipment we've got,

34
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:26,560
from expensive to the cheapest and smallest thing,

35
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,960
this is probably one of the best friends you could possibly have

36
00:03:28,961 --> 00:03:30,560
when you're on a rig fire.

37
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:32,380
It's a typical corrugated tin.

38
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,160
There's a lot of barns built out of it, same type material.

39
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,920
The shiny galvanized sheet metal reflects heat.

40
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:44,280
Firefighters also use it to protect equipment.

41
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:47,680
As long as you've got water and you've got a piece of tin,

42
00:03:47,681 --> 00:03:49,520
you can stand up and basically touch the fire.

43
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:55,140
There are many ways a disaster like a blowing, burning well could ignite.

44
00:03:56,580 --> 00:04:00,940
An errant spark, lightning, or the friction of blowing debris.

45
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:05,560
It can even happen before a well begins producing.

46
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:12,480
When sinking a well, drillers may have encountered greater than expected pressure

47
00:04:12,481 --> 00:04:14,580
from the oil reservoir trapped underground,

48
00:04:14,581 --> 00:04:18,580
leading to the failure of surface containment equipment.

49
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:23,620
When the flow of crude is uncontrolled, that's a blowout.

50
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,820
And at any time, that combustible oil or gas can catch fire.

51
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:33,060
Traditionally, one of the most effective ways to snuff a fire

52
00:04:33,061 --> 00:04:38,100
has been to literally blow it out using explosives.

53
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:42,100
It's simply robbing it of oxygen.

54
00:04:42,101 --> 00:04:47,460
You place that charge just near the base of the flame,

55
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:54,420
where the fluid coming from the well is just becoming oxygen-rich enough to burn.

56
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,800
And it's put in a so-called shot drum,

57
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:03,060
which is nothing more than either a 55-gallon or a 35-gallon barrel.

58
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:10,680
The technique of using explosives to blow out fires

59
00:05:10,681 --> 00:05:12,680
has been around since the 1920s.

60
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:19,560
Today, blowout specialists occasionally still use explosives,

61
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:22,800
but most prefer less destructive methods.

62
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,380
The same water pumps firefighters use to cool themselves

63
00:05:27,381 --> 00:05:29,200
put out the fires, too.

64
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:33,280
Today, when you go put 6,000, 8,000 gallons a minute,

65
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,300
you can extinguish just about anything

66
00:05:35,301 --> 00:05:37,580
with that kind of water supply in the right place.

67
00:05:37,581 --> 00:05:44,400
It takes a lot of the thrill out of it, but it's a little more practical, really.

68
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:48,780
But the specialists also have to cap the well.

69
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:55,540
Sometimes this is done while it's burning, if there's poisonous gas,

70
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:59,360
or it poses a larger environmental hazard to put it out.

71
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:06,700
Many times the team has to cut off a damaged wellhead before attaching a new control device.

72
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:16,300
Specialists often use a tool called an abrasive jet cutter to do this job.

73
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:23,000
The tool uses high-pressure water mixed with sand to sever the damaged wellhead.

74
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:34,360
Next, a capping stack with several blowout preventers, or BOPs, is forced over the blowing well and secured to the pipe.

75
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:41,620
BOPs are also used during drilling to control well pressure.

76
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:50,500
This type of BOP uses hydraulic rams that close and seal off the hole.

77
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:59,040
And when you actually get the pleasure of shutting the blowout preventers in,

78
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:00,460
it's the quietest quiet in the world.

79
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,020
It's a very good feeling.

80
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,040
Clearly, it's a dangerous line of work.

81
00:07:06,041 --> 00:07:12,360
and it always has been

82
00:07:12,361 --> 00:07:15,960
thousands of people have died fighting these fires over the past century and a

83
00:07:15,961 --> 00:07:18,800
half

84
00:07:18,801 --> 00:07:22,800
in August 1859 oil speculator Edwin Drake

85
00:07:22,801 --> 00:07:27,220
drilled the first oil well in the United States

86
00:07:27,221 --> 00:07:32,280
two months later it caught fire fire becomes a problem in the oil industry

87
00:07:32,281 --> 00:07:33,920
as soon as you bring oil to the surface.

88
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:38,520
It's so combustible, and in the rush to develop,

89
00:07:39,100 --> 00:07:42,080
they spread oil all over everything, as far as you can see,

90
00:07:42,140 --> 00:07:43,560
getting that oil out as fast as they can,

91
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:45,860
setting the stage for serious fires

92
00:07:45,861 --> 00:07:47,560
any time there's any kind of a spark.

93
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:52,040
The invention of the automobile

94
00:07:52,041 --> 00:07:54,900
further fueled demand for petroleum exploration.

95
00:07:56,740 --> 00:07:59,740
Huge reserves discovered in places like Beaumont, Texas,

96
00:07:59,741 --> 00:08:01,980
in 1901 at Spindletop

97
00:08:01,981 --> 00:08:04,480
ushered in the age of the gusher.

98
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,840
A gusher occurs when drillers hit a greater than expected pocket of oil or gas,

99
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:14,940
and oil shoots back up the wellbore uncontrolled.

100
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:18,540
To add to the fire hazard,

101
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:24,040
well owners initially stored oil in open pits before storage tanks were built.

102
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:28,580
In the early fields all the way into the 1950s,

103
00:08:28,581 --> 00:08:34,400
Most of the fields burned, in part at least, when they were developed.

104
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:36,640
It was just almost impossible to keep this from happening.

105
00:08:37,660 --> 00:08:44,060
But in 1922, Cameron Ironworks invented the first effective blowout preventer,

106
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:48,560
which reduced blowouts and made drilling operations safer.

107
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:52,820
Fewer blowouts meant fewer fires.

108
00:08:52,821 --> 00:08:58,640
Conversely, if a well wasn't producing as much as the owner thought it was capable of,

109
00:08:58,641 --> 00:09:03,820
a specialized workman called a torpedo shooter was called in.

110
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:12,880
Torpedo shooters dropped explosive nitroglycerin canisters called torpedoes down the well to induce flow.

111
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:22,660
They would literally drop that down the well bore to fracture the rock down there or the sand so that it could produce more.

112
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:26,900
That was an interesting group of men.

113
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:34,920
I think it was once said that two out of three torpedo shooters died before they were 30 years old.

114
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:36,960
It was a terribly dangerous line of work.

115
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:42,200
One of those daring oil field workers was a shooter named Carl Kinley.

116
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:49,060
Carl was a widower who worked in the oil fields of central California to support his three children.

117
00:09:49,061 --> 00:09:59,060
on april 29th 1913 carl was helping clear a valve off a damaged oil rig from a small fire

118
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:04,640
until this time no one had found a very effective way of snuffing these fires

119
00:10:04,641 --> 00:10:13,760
they felt like they may have a way of controlling the fire with steam if they were to get the fire

120
00:10:13,761 --> 00:10:15,342
going straight up.

121
00:10:15,343 --> 00:10:22,200
And so the procedure called for using explosives to blow a valve off the well.

122
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:27,840
Carl loaded some nitroglycerin and carefully placed it by the rig.

123
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:33,800
But when he detonated the nitro, an amazing thing happened. The fire went out.

124
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:40,860
Moments later, the oil reignited. No one thought much of the fire having gone out,

125
00:10:40,861 --> 00:10:44,820
except for Carl's oldest son, Myron, who was standing nearby.

126
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:51,020
14-year-old Myron Kinley knew he had just witnessed something important.

127
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:58,180
Little did he know, he'd seen the birth of a multi-million dollar industry he'd soon helped create.

128
00:11:00,620 --> 00:11:05,680
In the late 19th century, the main product of petroleum oil was kerosene,

129
00:11:05,681 --> 00:11:09,800
which competed with whale oil as the prime source of illumination.

130
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:15,860
Oil firefighting will return on Modern Marvels.

131
00:11:18,860 --> 00:11:28,040
Myron Kinley, middle school dropout and oil patch shooter's assistant, almost single-handedly created the modern oil firefighting industry.

132
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:37,280
The concept simmered in his mind after he witnessed his father inadvertently blow out a well fire using explosives.

133
00:11:37,281 --> 00:11:41,520
But it didn't happen overnight.

134
00:11:42,840 --> 00:11:47,040
First, Myron saw action during the First World War in an artillery unit,

135
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:50,480
gaining experience on how to set explosive charges.

136
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:55,080
After the war, he joined his younger brother Floyd

137
00:11:55,081 --> 00:11:58,380
in a marginally successful torpedo shooting business,

138
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:01,540
using explosives to stimulate well production.

139
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:06,820
But one event would change the brother's business plan.

140
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:20,280
In 1924, Myron and Floyd watched as men struggled for 12 days to put out a burning well in Cromwell, Oklahoma, using water and dirt.

141
00:12:20,281 --> 00:12:34,280
Remembering Carl's example 11 years earlier, Myron offered to put the fire out in two days for the then huge sum of $500.

142
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:43,160
Myron realized his father's earlier torpedo had sucked up all the available oxygen, suffocating the fire.

143
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:54,500
If Myron could just build a big enough torpedo, he could blow out the Cromwell fire and prevent it from immediately reigniting.

144
00:12:56,500 --> 00:13:00,480
The well owner agreed to the deal without expecting the crazy notion to work.

145
00:13:01,780 --> 00:13:06,280
But Myron proved his theory with a terrific explosion.

146
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:11,100
At that moment, he knew he'd created his new line of work.

147
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:18,100
He soon quit torpedo shooting and became a full-time oil well firefighter.

148
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:29,460
By 1931, his business had grown to a point that he moved from Tulsa to Houston,

149
00:13:29,461 --> 00:13:33,960
the center of the oil well service industry.

150
00:13:33,961 --> 00:13:39,340
Within a few years, Myron and Floyd Kinley were legendary.

151
00:13:40,660 --> 00:13:45,020
Oil men all over the world knew to call Kinley if they had a blowout.

152
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:52,660
The Kinley brothers honed their technique for dousing and capping fires throughout the late 20s and 30s.

153
00:13:54,360 --> 00:14:00,020
When they worked together, it was a very businesslike atmosphere.

154
00:14:00,021 --> 00:14:04,820
and one person was in charge, whether it was my father or my uncle.

155
00:14:05,460 --> 00:14:07,680
They had the utmost respect for one another,

156
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:11,320
and I think they cared deeply about one another.

157
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:18,740
The Kinleys found that relatively lightweight sheets of corrugated galvanized tin

158
00:14:18,741 --> 00:14:21,240
deflected heat better than other materials.

159
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:28,900
The Kinleys also used hoses to create a constant and drenching artificial rain,

160
00:14:28,901 --> 00:14:31,420
keeping men and equipment from incinerating.

161
00:14:33,300 --> 00:14:37,400
The water also lessened chances of a spark reigniting a snuffed well.

162
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:43,420
For this reason, the site around a burning well was always raked clean of debris

163
00:14:43,421 --> 00:14:46,820
so that nothing hot could start another fire.

164
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:53,060
Though the process was slow and tedious, it meant life or death for the Kinleys.

165
00:14:53,860 --> 00:14:57,180
I think you've got to clear away all the debris away from the wellhead

166
00:14:57,181 --> 00:14:59,120
and protect that wellhead at all times

167
00:14:59,121 --> 00:15:00,700
because that's what you want to work on.

168
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:04,680
When it came time to actually blow out the well,

169
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:07,940
Myram packed explosives into a shot canister.

170
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:12,240
Dynamite caps were inserted into the shot

171
00:15:12,241 --> 00:15:14,820
to detonate the load when they had it where they wanted it.

172
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:21,160
The canister was then delicately placed next to the burning fire.

173
00:15:23,660 --> 00:15:25,780
Initially, the brothers carried the explosives

174
00:15:25,781 --> 00:15:27,380
up to the mouth of the burning well,

175
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:30,720
set it down under a constant stream of water,

176
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:34,280
and ran like hell before the canister was detonated.

177
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:39,000
Of course, the Kinley brothers were only half finished

178
00:15:39,001 --> 00:15:39,980
when the fire was out.

179
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:42,520
Most people don't understand.

180
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:45,140
Getting rid of the fire is the easiest thing, hell, you know.

181
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:47,880
But you've got to cap the well because it's still blowing.

182
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:48,960
It just isn't on fire.

183
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:50,840
And that's where the work comes in.

184
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:54,580
Under a spray of volatile oil or gas,

185
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:57,600
The Kinleys cut off the damaged wellhead.

186
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:01,100
On this well, they used metal hacksaws.

187
00:16:02,780 --> 00:16:08,540
They had to be careful, as any spark could reignite the well, incinerating the men.

188
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:14,420
The brothers then installed a new series of valves known as a Christmas tree.

189
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,860
Finally, they shut the new Christmas tree, capping the gusher.

190
00:16:19,861 --> 00:16:25,200
Myron and Floyd were always careful

191
00:16:25,201 --> 00:16:28,600
but sometimes the unexpected happened

192
00:16:28,601 --> 00:16:32,840
in 1929 near Gladewater, Texas

193
00:16:32,841 --> 00:16:34,420
Myron got hurt

194
00:16:34,421 --> 00:16:39,380
he was hooking a cable onto debris

195
00:16:39,381 --> 00:16:41,460
when his leg was trapped

196
00:16:41,461 --> 00:16:43,380
he could have very easily burned up

197
00:16:43,381 --> 00:16:46,300
had his brother Floyd not been there very quickly

198
00:16:46,301 --> 00:16:48,400
to take him out of that situation

199
00:16:48,401 --> 00:16:50,100
that he saved his life.

200
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:55,360
Myron was fortunate to escape with only a broken leg.

201
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:00,060
The brothers discovered the hard way,

202
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:01,820
what worked and what didn't.

203
00:17:03,100 --> 00:17:06,020
They've had a monopoly, if you will,

204
00:17:06,100 --> 00:17:09,840
on the business of oil welfare fighting all those years.

205
00:17:11,460 --> 00:17:13,440
But success had its price.

206
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:17,960
In 1939, the dangers of the profession

207
00:17:17,961 --> 00:17:19,720
struck home for the Kindley family.

208
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:23,320
Oh, his brother, Floyd, got killed at

209
00:17:23,321 --> 00:17:25,040
in South Texas somewhere,

210
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:26,240
backing off some drill pipe,

211
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:27,160
and it had come loose,

212
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:28,580
and that's what hit him in the head

213
00:17:28,581 --> 00:17:29,281
and killed him.

214
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:31,960
Myron was battling another fire,

215
00:17:32,580 --> 00:17:34,080
too far away to save Floyd,

216
00:17:34,380 --> 00:17:36,420
the way Floyd had saved him years earlier.

217
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:39,480
It was a terrible tragedy,

218
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:40,420
the whole family.

219
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:43,900
Floyd and Myron were very close.

220
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:47,200
It was a terrible thing.

221
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:52,440
Myron's wife had the sad task of telling Floyd's wife the news.

222
00:17:54,420 --> 00:17:57,900
After dinner, I was sent out with someone to go to a movie

223
00:17:57,901 --> 00:18:00,360
so that Aunt Marguerite would be there.

224
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:02,220
The house would be quiet.

225
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:08,000
Mother took that way of telling Aunt Marguerite about the loss of her husband.

226
00:18:08,680 --> 00:18:13,680
Aunt Marguerite thought she was going to see him the next day,

227
00:18:13,681 --> 00:18:16,140
you know, go down there and all, and he's dead.

228
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:24,080
With Floyd dead, Myron needed an experienced partner to help him with all the business

229
00:18:24,081 --> 00:18:24,800
coming his way.

230
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:32,580
It seemed as if there was nobody else with the skills, bravery, and foolhardiness necessary.

231
00:18:33,460 --> 00:18:39,140
That is, until the greatest oil well firefighter who ever lived walked into Myron Kinley's

232
00:18:39,141 --> 00:18:40,880
office looking for a job.

233
00:18:40,881 --> 00:18:48,840
In 1931, Myron Kinley battled an oil well fire in Romania that had burned for more than

234
00:18:48,841 --> 00:18:49,541
two years.

235
00:18:49,542 --> 00:18:54,640
Myron capped the well within three months, capturing international attention.

236
